   109	b0(B)=1,                 S0=empty,                 Q=0,             (2.1)
   110	R -> B finite and point-bijective, hence an analytic homeomorphism, (2.2)
   111	every component normalization is A1, and the affine incidence graph
   112	of R (with loops and parallel edges retained) is a forest.          (2.3)
   113	```
   114	
   115	Contractibility does not follow from `e_c(B)=1` alone.  It follows from the
   116	whole package (2.1)--(2.3): the analytic homeomorphism transfers the source
   117	topology to `B`; each normalized `A1` component is contractible; unibranch
   118	singularities do not alter its component topology; and every multibranch
   119	identification is recorded by the connected forest incidence graph.  Thus
   120	
   121	```text
