   100	Cohen--Macaulay, so miracle flatness over the regular target makes `g2`
   101	finite flat; integrality makes it surjective.
   102	
   103	The non-obvious strengthening is completion rigidity. At a closed point
   104	`x`, with `y=g1(x)` and `z=F(x)`, étaleness of `F` identifies the completed
   105	local rings at `z` and `x`. Quasi-finiteness of `g1` makes the completed
   106	local ring at `x` finite over that at `y`. Excellence and normality make the
   107	completed local ring at `y` a two-dimensional normal domain. The intervening
   108	map has height-zero kernel and is surjective because its composite is the
   109	completion isomorphism; it is therefore itself an isomorphism. Flatness and
   110	unramifiedness descend, so both `g1` at `x` and `g2` at `y` are étale. Since
   111	the schemes are Jacobson, this holds everywhere on the image. Thus the image
   112	of `g1` is open and disjoint from `R` and from `Sing(Y)`.
   113	
   114	If `R` were empty, the connected finite cover `g2` would be finite étale over
   115	`A^2_C`, hence trivial, contradicting `d2>=2`. Purity (equivalently here the
   116	finite-flat discriminant support together with branch purity) makes the
   117	nonempty branch locus divisorial. This proves that the codimension-one image
   118	antecedent fails rather than remaining an open bridge.
   119	
   120	Finite `g2` is proper, which gives the composition identity
   121	`A(F)=g2(A(g1))`. Openness and density of `g1(A^2)`, plus compactness of
   122	bounded subsets of `A^2(C)`, give `Y minus g1(A^2) subset A(g1)`. Since all
   123	of `R` is missed, its discriminant image lies in `A(F)`.
   124	
   125	The fibre deficit needs one normalization lemma that was implicit in the
   126	review. Let `Z` be the normalization of `Y` in `C(x,y)`; strong Zariski Main
   127	embeds `A^2` openly in `Z`. For a closed `y in Y`, localize at `y` and let
   128	`q_1,...,q_r` be the points of `Z` above it. Their residue fields are `C`.
   129	Chinese remaindering supplies an integral element `b` with pairwise distinct
   130	residues at the `q_i`. Its minimal polynomial over `K` has degree at most
   131	`d1` and coefficients in the normal local ring: integrality makes those
   132	coefficients integral, and normality brings them back into the ring. Modulo
   133	the maximal ideal this polynomial has the `r` distinct chosen residues as
   134	roots, so `r<=d1`. Thus every `g1` fibre has at most `d1` points. A finite-
   135	flat `g2` fibre has at most `d2` distinct points, and over its discriminant at
   136	least one lies in `R` and contributes no `g1` preimage. This proves the
   137	displayed `deg(F)-d1` bound. Outside `A(F)`, properness over a small
   138	neighborhood makes the étale map `F` a covering of its generic degree,
   139	giving equality.
   140	
   141	Finally, if `Y` were abstractly `A^2`, the factorization could be conjugated
   142	to polynomial self-maps `F=G2 o G1` of `A^2`. The chain rule makes both
   143	Jacobian determinants nonzero constants (the image of `G1` is dense), so
   144	the finite map `G2` would be a connected finite étale cover of degree
   145	`d2>=2` of `A^2_C`, again impossible.
   146	
   147	## 3. Binding corrections and withheld claims
   148	
   149	1. The launch condition "`g1(A^2)` contains every codimension-one point" is
   150	   refuted for every nontrivial block: the whole nonempty branch divisor is
   151	   missed. It must not remain listed as an attainable proof obligation.
   152	2. The alternative recognition target `Y isomorphic to A^2` is also
   153	   impossible for every nontrivial block. It is a reductio, not a descent
   154	   theorem waiting to be proved.
   155	3. Consequently this route gives no implication from minimal topological
   156	   degree to primitive monodromy. Primitivity remains open. The honest
   157	   successor is to rule out the forced étale sandwiches directly, beginning
   158	   provisionally with block index `d2=2` (`BD-D2`).
   159	4. The review's estimate `#F^{-1}(z) <= deg(F)-d1` is promoted only with the
   160	   CRT/minimal-polynomial normalization lemma supplied in section 2. It does
   161	   not require flatness of the degree-`d1` normalization and makes no
   162	   attainment claim beyond the displayed upper bound.
   163	5. The stronger wording that discriminant components are polynomially
   164	   parametrized components of `A(F)` is citation-dependent and unnecessary
   165	   for the structural result. Only the proved inclusion
   166	   `g2(R)=V(Disc(g2)) subset A(F)` is promoted here.
   167	
   168	## 4. Scope and next discriminator
   169	
   170	The theorem constrains only a block that already exists. It does not supply
   171	an intermediate field, prove primitive monodromy, select a bounded
   172	topological degree, control all components of `A(F)`, decide smoothness of
   173	`Y`, or prove JC2. The proposed next discriminator `BD-D2` is not promoted:
   174	it asks whether a degree-two finite-flat branched cover in this sandwich can
   175	be ruled out using the topology of the complement and the geometry at
   176	infinity. It requires its own producer and hostile review.
